Title: run_corpus_pipeline.sh auto-commits and pushes dvc.lock straight to main
Created: 2026-07-27
Author: HDMX-coding-agent

--- log ---
2026-07-27T14:40Z HDMX-coding-agent created found while scoping 0336: the sign-off invariant Flag 5 depends on is not operationalized

--- body ---
## Context

`scripts/run_corpus_pipeline.sh` ends by committing and pushing `dvc.lock`
directly to `main`:

```
Auto-committing dvc.lock...
git add dvc.lock
git commit -m "data: update dvc.lock after pipeline re-run"
git push origin main
```

Two problems.

**It bypasses the branch-and-PR gate.** Every other change in this repo lands
via branch and merge request; `main` is read-only by convention. This script
writes to it directly, so a pipeline re-run mutates `main` with no review and no
record beyond a generic commit message. `main` is unprotected and there is no
CI, so nothing stops it.

**It removes the checkpoint that pending corpus changes rely on.** Ticket 0361
holds Flag 5 inert behind `--skip-semantic-flag` precisely because activating it
would remove 361 works and needs author sign-off. But any config or code change
that alters the corpus is executed and published by the next `make corpus` with
no confirmation step. The sign-off invariant is written in the tickets and
nowhere in the machinery.

Discovered while scoping 0336; the implementing agent flagged it and the
behaviour was verified by reading the script.

## Relevant files

- `scripts/run_corpus_pipeline.sh` — the auto-commit block (around lines 59-89)
- `Makefile` — the `corpus` target invoking it
- `dvc.lock` — what gets pushed

## Actions

1. Replace the direct push with either a branch plus merge request, or a loud
   refusal that prints the diff and exits non-zero when `dvc.lock` changed,
   leaving the commit to a human.
2. Keep the existing "files other than dvc.lock changed" warning path — it is
   already the right instinct, applied to the wrong branch target.
3. Decide whether an explicit `--publish` opt-in is warranted for the case where
   an operator does want the lock committed in the same run.

## Test

- An integration test driving the script with a modified `dvc.lock` in a
  throwaway repo, asserting that `main` is unchanged and the exit status is
  non-zero (or that a branch was created), never that `origin/main` advanced.

## Verification

- [ ] A pipeline re-run that changes `dvc.lock` does not advance `main`
- [ ] The operator gets an actionable message naming what changed
- [ ] `make corpus` still succeeds end to end on padme

## Invariants

- The corpus is frozen (author, 2026-07-27) mid RDJ-26561 R&R. This ticket
  touches the pipeline entry point, so land it when a corpus run is not in
  flight, and do not trigger a rebuild to test it.
- Do not weaken the existing warning path.

## Exit criteria

- A corpus re-run can no longer publish to `main` on its own, and a pending
  corpus change cannot be executed without an explicit human step.
